  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Rotating Phasors",
    "lesson_core_move": "Track a phasor as uniform rotation in the complex plane by computing its instantaneous angle from initial phase and angular frequency.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Rotating Phasors",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Rotating Phasors",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A rotating phasor is a complex number with fixed magnitude whose angle advances uniformly with time. The cue is that only the angle changes; the length stays constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta\\theta=\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the simplest base case, $A=1$ and $\\phi=0$. Then elapsed time alone determines the angle, so the rotation after time $t$ is $\\theta(t)=\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A nonzero initial phase $\\phi$ just shifts the starting angle. The instantaneous angle is the initial phase plus the elapsed rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\phi+\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the phasor itself by putting that angle into the complex exponential. This keeps the magnitude fixed and lets the angle advance uniformly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "z(t)=Ae^{j\\theta(t)}=Ae^{j(\\phi+\\omega t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "At $t=0$, the formula returns the initial phasor $z(0)=Ae^{j\\phi}$. That confirms the rule: the angle starts at $\\phi$ and then increases by $\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "z(0)=Ae^{j\\phi}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Rotating Phasors",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing Rotation Angle from Elapsed Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor starts at angle $0$ and rotates at angular frequency $\\omega=2$ rad/s. What angle has it rotated through after $t=3$ s?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a phasor with zero initial phase, so the angle after elapsed time comes only from the rotation amount. The rule is the product $\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta\\theta=\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given angular frequency and elapsed time: $\\omega=2$ rad/s and $t=3$ s. Then multiply to find the rotation amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Delta\\theta=(2)(3)=6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\phi=0$, the instantaneous angle is the same as the elapsed rotation. So after $3$ s, the phasor has rotated through $6$ radians.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Rotating Phasors",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Adding Initial Phase to the Rotation Angle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor has initial phase $\\phi=\\pi/3$ and rotates at angular frequency $\\omega=2\\pi$ rad/s. What is its instantaneous angle after $t=\\tfrac{1}{4}$ s?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a nonzero initial phase, so the instantaneous angle must include both the starting angle and the elapsed rotation. Use the same rule from the previous step: $\\theta(t)=\\phi+\\omega t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\phi+\\omega t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given values $\\phi=\\pi/3$, $\\omega=2\\pi$, and $t=\\tfrac{1}{4}$. Then compute the elapsed rotation first so the addition stays clean.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\frac{\\pi}{3}+(2\\pi)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{3}+\\frac{\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Add the angles to get a single instantaneous angle. The phasor is at $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ after $\\tfrac{1}{4}$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\theta(t)=\\frac{5\\pi}{6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Rotating Phasors",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the Instantaneous Phasor from Its Angle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor has magnitude $A=3$ and instantaneous angle $5\\pi/6$. Write the phasor in complex exponential form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the magnitude and angle of the phasor. For a phasor, use the standard complex exponential form $z=Ae^{j\\theta}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "z=Ae^{j\\theta}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given magnitude $A=3$ and angle $\\theta=5\\pi/6$ directly into the form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "z=3e^{j5\\pi/6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the instantaneous phasor in complex exponential form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Rotating Phasors",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reducing a Phasor Angle After Full Rotations",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A phasor has angle $11\\pi/3$. Reduce it to an equivalent angle in $[0,2\\pi)$ and write the corresponding phasor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is an angle that is larger than one full turn. Phasor angles repeat every $2\\pi$, so subtract full $2\\pi$ turns until the angle lies in $[0,2\\pi)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{11\\pi}{3}-2\\pi=\\frac{11\\pi}{3}-\\frac{6\\pi}{3}=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The reduced angle $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$ is in the requested interval and is coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi}{3}$. The phasor itself does not change; only the angle label changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "z=e^{j11\\pi/3}=e^{j5\\pi/3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the equivalent angle is $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$, and the same phasor can be written with that reduced angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
